sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.39, "baseline_margin": 3.81, "analyst_adjustment": 0.69, "key_factors": ["Democratic-favorable national environment in a state that normally leans Republican", "Fragmented Republican field under ranked-choice voting, with a late consolidation push", "Kreiss-Tomkins' fundraising edge and outside-group backing versus GOP counter-spending", "Thin, mixed polling with large undecided share"], "rationale": "Recent surveys, including from Republican-affiliated pollsters, show Kreiss-Tomkins ahead in an open-seat race, markets price the Democrat a clear favorite, and raters split between toss-up and lean-Democratic. The Republican rank-1-2-3 consolidation effort and late national money on both sides keep this closer than the polling alone suggests.", "watch": "Whether the GOP consolidation behind Taylor, backed by fresh national spending, moves enough undecided voters in the final weeks to close the gap.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike Dunleavy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alaska's governorship is open as Republican Mike Dunleavy terms out, and the contest pairs Democrat Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins against Republicans Treg Taylor, Bernadette Wilson and Dave Bronson under ranked-choice voting. The state leans Republican, but the national climate has shifted toward Democrats, and recent surveys, including ones from Republican-affiliated pollsters, show Kreiss-Tomkins ahead, with many voters still undecided.\n\nNonpartisan raters split between toss-up and lean-Democratic, and prediction markets make the Democrat a clear favorite. Kreiss-Tomkins has out-raised the field, though Taylor holds more cash on hand, and national money is flowing to both sides. Republicans are urging their voters to rank the three G.O.P. candidates ahead of the Democrat, a consolidation push that could tighten the race in the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6825, "margin": 2.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem